Fix a matrix norm bound. For the covariance conclusion, assume positive margins in \eqref{prof:eq26} and suppose that every point in its small-residual region has \(q(y)\ge c_*>0\). On the fixed-length ordinary and restricted inverse-word events specified below, \[ \|\operatorname{Cov}_{\mu_h}(G,X)\| \le C igl(\operatorname{Var}_{\mu_h}(G)+\mathcal D_h(G) igr)^{1/2} \quad	ext{for every }G. \] In particular $\|\Sigma_h\|\le C$. For the following mean conclusion, only the whole-region root stability implication \[ \|F_h(y)\|\leho_0\sqrt n \quad\Longrightarrow\quad \mathcal H(y,V_y)\succeq c_0I \] is required, together with ordinary word diagnostics. No lower bound on \(q(y)\) is assumed in this mean conclusion. For $N=\mu_h(f^2)>0$ and $
u_h=f^2\mu_h/N$, and each sufficiently small fixed $\zeta>0$,  egin{equation}  rac{\|\mathbb E_{
u_h}X-\mathbb E_{\mu_h}X\|}{\sqrt n} \le C\zeta+ rac{C_\zeta}{\sqrt n} \left(1+ rac{\mathcal D_h(f)}Night). \end{equation} The leading constant \(C\) depends on the fixed norm and stability margins, but not on \(\zeta\). Without the stability hypothesis, put \[ Q_\delta(h)=\sup_{\|F_h(y)\|\le\delta\sqrt n}q(y), \] with an empty supremum equal to zero. The same ordinary diagnostics give  egin{equation}  rac{\|\mathbb E_{
u_h}X-\mathbb E_{\mu_h}X\|}{\sqrt n} \le 2\sqrt{Q_\delta(h)}+  rac{C_\delta}{\sqrt n} \left(1+ rac{\mathcal D_h(f)}Night). \end{equation} For each fixed $\delta>0$ this assertion holds for sufficiently large $n$ on the corresponding ordinary diagnostics. All events are independent of $f,G$ and the unit directions used to test these inequalities.
